"ref": {
"pA": 0.505,
"detail": {
"kind": "open",
"reference": "A poetic, introspective Elden Ring fanfiction following the Tarnished and featuring Melina, Ranni, Godrick, Malenia, and the Fire Giant. Explore their conflicts, choice, fate, and power; present the Golden Order, Age of Stars, and Frenzied Flame endings with moral nuance.",
"a": 0.97,
"b": 0.96
},
"calls": {
"jev": 2,
"luna": 1
},
"usd": 8.5e-05,
"seconds": {
"jev": 1.253,
"luna": 2.371
},
"missing": 0
},
"adv": {
"pA": 0.5449999999999999,
"detail": {
"a": {
"objection": "Ranni’s plan is misrepresented as abolishing rules and predetermined paths; in the game, she removes Order from direct worldly reach, making it distant and uncertain—not eliminating Order or guaranteeing self-determination.",
"p": 0.73
},
"b": {
"objection": "The Frenzied Flame ending has Melina consumed with the world, but in the game she survives that ending and vows to hunt the Tarnished; this reverses a key consequence of the choice.",
"p": 0.82
}
},
"calls": {
"jev": 2,
"luna": 2
},
"usd": 0.001398,
"seconds": {
"jev": 1.914,
"luna": 20.913
},
"missing": 0
},
"debate": {
"pA": 0.53,
"detail": {
"AB": 0.45,
"BA": 0.39,
"first_bias": -0.15999999999999992,
"split": true,
"cases": {
"a": "MINE is stronger because it develops each character’s inner conflict in depth: Melina questions a life defined by sacrifice, Ranni doubts whether cosmic exile is freedom, and Godrick recognizes the terror beneath his ambition. Its three endings also examine moral costs—restored order’s safety, the Age of Stars’ uncertainty, and Frenzied Flame’s “mercy”—in a sustained, introspective poetic voice.",
"b": "MINE is the better fit because it directly gives the Tarnished’s journey a clear arc—from meeting Melina, Ranni, Godrick, Malenia, and the Fire Giant to choosing an ending—and explicitly explores all three requested outcomes. Its compact, poetic scenes make each conflict legible, while lines such as “Freedom is cold” and “Was this liberation… or surrender?” preserve moral ambiguity without the OTHER’s much longer digressions."
}
},
